# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Renal Social Worker self-assessment, Modules 01-10. Correct option first; quizutil.balance() spreads the keys."""

QUIZ = {
"01": [
    ("Who should sign a professional social work assessment such as a social case study?",
     ["The registered social worker who did the assessment", "A navigator who has completed this course", "Any staff member with chart access", "The billing clerk, if the social worker countersigns later"], 0,
     "Best: a signature states that the signer did the assessment, and professional reports belong to the responsible registered social worker. Why not the others: course completion confers no authority; chart access is not professional authority; a later countersignature would make the report false."),
    ("Before inviting a relative into an adult patient&rsquo;s interview, what should you do first?",
     ["Ask the patient privately whom they want involved and what may be shared", "Assume consent because the relative pays for care", "Give the relative a copy of the chart to save time", "Postpone the interview until the whole family can attend"], 0,
     "Best: an adult who can decide chooses who joins. Why not the others: paying for care is not permission; sharing the chart breaches privacy; waiting for the whole family delays help and still skips the patient&rsquo;s choice."),
    ("A pharmacy offers you a &ldquo;token&rdquo; for each patient you refer. What is the best response?",
     ["Decline, document it, and tell your supervisor", "Accept it if the patient still gets a good price", "Accept it but tell no one", "Ask the patient whether they mind"], 0,
     "Best: a referral benefit creates a conflict of interest that must be declined and managed through supervision. Why not the others: a good price does not remove the conflict; secrecy compounds it; the patient cannot waive your professional duty."),
    ("Which of these decisions belongs to the attending nephrologist, not the social worker?",
     ["The dialysis prescription and whether a session can be moved", "Exploring transport barriers", "Writing the psychosocial formulation", "Coordinating an assistance referral"], 0,
     "Best: treatment decisions are medical. Why not the others: transport, formulation, and assistance coordination are social work tasks."),
    ("What should you say before asking a new patient any personal question?",
     ["Your role, the purpose of the meeting, who may see the information, and the limits of confidentiality", "Nothing; explanations make people nervous", "Only your name", "That everything they say will stay secret"], 0,
     "Best: people share more safely when they know why you ask and who will see it. Why not the others: silence or a name alone leaves them uninformed; promising total secrecy is false because confidentiality has limits, such as immediate risk."),
    ("A navigator is asked to sign a social case study because the social worker is on leave. What should the navigator do?",
     ["Prepare the documents and call the covering registered social worker", "Sign it, since the social worker can countersign on Monday", "Refuse and tell the family to come back next month", "Ask the nurse to sign instead"], 0,
     "Best: the navigator can prepare everything and route the professional task to the covering social worker. Why not the others: signing is outside the navigator&rsquo;s authority; sending the family away abandons them; a nurse cannot sign a social work assessment either."),
    ("Which statement best describes the purpose of the renal social worker?",
     ["Helping people with kidney disease and their families address psychosocial and social barriers to the care they choose, starting from their goals", "Processing guarantee letters for the dialysis unit", "Persuading patients to follow the team&rsquo;s plan", "Deciding who is eligible for transplant"], 0,
     "Best: the role starts from the person&rsquo;s goal and covers assessment, support, and access. Why not the others: assistance paperwork is one tool, not the profession; persuasion on the team&rsquo;s behalf is not the role; transplant eligibility is the transplant team&rsquo;s decision."),
    ("A patient&rsquo;s family asks you not to tell him his kidneys will not recover. What is the best first step?",
     ["Explore the family&rsquo;s fear, involve the nephrologist, and ask the patient how much he wants to know", "Agree, because the family knows him best", "Tell the patient immediately to prove your independence", "Refuse to discuss it and end the meeting"], 0,
     "Best: respect the family&rsquo;s concern while protecting the adult patient&rsquo;s right to information, with the clinician who gives the news. Why not the others: agreeing sets aside the patient&rsquo;s rights; rushing ahead ignores the family and the nephrologist&rsquo;s role; ending the meeting solves nothing."),
    ("Why should a unit have a written role map with absence cover?",
     ["So that every recurring task has an owner, a backup, and an urgent route before anyone goes on leave", "Because the DOH licensing order sets a social worker staffing ratio", "To decide which staff member is most important", "So navigators can do professional tasks when needed"], 0,
     "Best: written roles stop work from flowing to whoever is nearest. Why not the others: the reviewed DOH order sets no social worker ratio; the map is about tasks, not importance; it keeps professional tasks with professionals."),
    ("During a routine benefit request, the charge nurse reports that the patient said she &ldquo;cannot keep doing this&rdquo; and cried. What comes first?",
     ["Tell the attending physician and the covering social worker today, before the paperwork", "Finish the benefit application, then ask about it next week", "Assume she means the paperwork is stressful", "Ask the daughter what her mother meant"], 0,
     "Best: a possible safety or stop-treatment statement comes before forms and goes to the clinician and social worker the same day. Why not the others: delay risks missing danger; assuming the meaning skips the question; the daughter cannot answer for her."),
],
"02": [
    ("Why is the two-day weekend gap the riskiest part of a three-times-a-week dialysis schedule?",
     ["Fluid and potassium keep building up between sessions, and the long gap lets them build longest", "Dialysis machines work less well on Mondays", "Patients eat less on weekends", "Staff are fewer on Mondays"], 0,
     "Best: without kidney function, water and potassium accumulate between sessions, most over the longest gap; a large cohort found more deaths on the day after it. Why not the others: the machines do not change; weekend eating usually adds, not reduces, load; staffing is not the mechanism."),
    ("A patient wants to skip Friday dialysis for a family trip. What is your role?",
     ["Bring the question to the nephrologist today and work on the travel arrangements", "Approve it if the trip is important", "Tell him skipping one session is harmless", "Tell him he cannot travel"], 0,
     "Best: the schedule is a medical decision; your work is the social part (temporary dialysis elsewhere, papers, cost). Why not the others: approval is not yours to give; one missed session is not harmless; forbidding travel is neither your decision nor the goal."),
    ("Severe breathlessness appears during a social work interview. What should happen?",
     ["Immediate clinical escalation to the nurse or physician", "Finish the interview first", "Advise the patient to skip the next session", "Book a routine follow-up next month"], 0,
     "Best: breathlessness can mean fluid in the lungs and needs clinical assessment now. Why not the others: finishing first delays care; skipping would worsen fluid overload; a routine booking ignores the urgency."),
    ("A patient asks which dialysis type is medically best for her. What should you do?",
     ["Arrange a clinical conversation with the nephrologist and explore her practical preferences", "Choose the cheapest option for her", "Promise she can have home dialysis", "Pick the option you think suits her life"], 0,
     "Best: medical suitability is the clinician&rsquo;s; you bring her life and preferences to the decision. Why not the others: cost alone is not the decision; promises outrun clinical assessment; choosing for her removes her voice."),
    ("Which statement about conservative kidney management is accurate?",
     ["It is active care focused on symptoms and the person&rsquo;s goals, without dialysis", "It means the team stops caring for the patient", "It is a cure that avoids dialysis", "It is only an insurance category"], 0,
     "Best: conservative management is planned, active care. Why not the others: the team continues to care; it is not a cure; it is a clinical pathway, not a billing label."),
    ("Which kidney job explains why many people on dialysis also need injections for anemia?",
     ["Making the hormone that tells the bone marrow to produce red cells", "Removing excess water", "Balancing potassium", "Removing urea"], 0,
     "Best: failing kidneys make too little of this hormone, which dialysis cannot replace. Why not the others: water, potassium, and urea removal are partly done by dialysis and do not cause anemia this way."),
    ("In the DOPPS cohort, how was skipping one or more sessions a month related to mortality?",
     ["It was associated with higher mortality (relative risk 1.30), an observational finding", "It was proven to cause a 30% rise in deaths", "It had no relationship with mortality", "It lowered mortality by reducing treatment burden"], 0,
     "Best: DOPPS is observational, so the right verb is &ldquo;associated with.&rdquo; Why not the others: an observational cohort cannot prove causation; the association was present; there was no protective effect."),
    ("A patient stopped her phosphate binders because they cost more than her rice. What is the best response?",
     ["Tell the nurse and nephrologist, and work on the medicine cost yourself", "Tell her the binders are not important", "Tell her to restart them immediately", "Record it and do nothing more"], 0,
     "Best: the clinical decision goes to the clinician; the cost problem stays with you. Why not the others: judging the medicine&rsquo;s importance or giving instructions is medical advice; doing nothing leaves both problems unsolved."),
    ("Why might a patient&rsquo;s tiredness and poor concentration be misread as low motivation?",
     ["Uremia, anemia, and poor sleep in kidney failure cause these symptoms too", "Patients on dialysis are usually unmotivated", "Dialysis removes motivation hormones", "Tiredness always means depression"], 0,
     "Best: kidney failure itself causes fatigue and poor focus, so describe what you see and let clinicians sort the cause. Why not the others: it is a stereotype; there is no such hormone; tiredness has many causes."),
    ("For most people who can have one, how does a transplant compare with dialysis, and how strong is the evidence?",
     ["It is associated with longer life and better quality of life, in many cohort studies; no trial has randomized the two", "It is proven by large randomized trials to double survival", "It offers no advantage over dialysis", "It is better for everyone, including frail older people"], 0,
     "Best: consistent cohort evidence, but observational. Why not the others: there are no such randomized trials; cohorts consistently favor transplant; it is not right or possible for everyone."),
],
"03": [
    ("Does a United States rule on dialysis social worker qualifications apply in the Philippines?",
     ["No; it is a foreign rule for that setting, so check Philippine law and local requirements", "Yes, for all dialysis facilities", "Yes, if a course cites it", "Yes, if a manager prefers it"], 0,
     "Best: jurisdiction matters; foreign rules are comparisons, not local requirements. Why not the others: citation or preference does not create legal authority."),
    ("A licensing checklist could not be retrieved. How should you record it?",
     ["As evidence still needing retrieval and verification", "As fully verified", "As irrelevant", "As proof that no requirement exists"], 0,
     "Best: an inaccessible document is an open task. Why not the others: you cannot verify what you did not read, and absence of access is not absence of a rule."),
    ("Which of these is an Academy design choice, not a legal requirement?",
     ["The course&rsquo;s 60-hour learning plan", "The existence of Republic Act No. 4373", "A published PhilHealth circular", "The Data Privacy Act"], 0,
     "Best: the hours are an educational plan. Why not the others: the law, the circular, and the Data Privacy Act are issued rules."),
    ("What is the most defensible basis for proposing social work staffing for a dialysis unit?",
     ["Demand and complexity in hours, compared with available hours and confirmed local rules", "A ratio copied from another country", "The number of dialysis chairs only", "A foreign salary scale"], 0,
     "Best: workload in hours reflects the actual work and local rules. Why not the others: a borrowed ratio has no local authority; chairs ignore complexity; salaries are irrelevant to demand."),
    ("A clinical head says, &ldquo;DOH did not require a social worker.&rdquo; Which reply is most accurate?",
     ["&ldquo;The DOH dialysis order we reviewed does not name a social worker post or ratio; the full current checklist should be confirmed.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;DOH requires one social worker per 50 patients.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;DOH prohibits social workers in dialysis.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;No rule anywhere requires social work.&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: a bounded finding states exactly what was reviewed. Why not the others: no such ratio was identified; nothing prohibits social work; &ldquo;no rule anywhere&rdquo; overstates a limited review."),
    ("When should you check a rule&rsquo;s effective date?",
     ["Every time, because publication and effectivity can differ", "Only for laws, not circulars", "Only when a patient complains", "Never; the publication date is enough"], 0,
     "Best: rules can be published before they take effect and may have transition provisions. Why not the others: circulars change often; waiting for complaints is too late; publication is not effectivity."),
    ("Which belongs on a one-page social work service charter?",
     ["Purpose, referral criteria, response targets, coverage, measures, and what the service does not do", "The social worker&rsquo;s salary", "Patients&rsquo; names and diagnoses", "A guarantee of same-day assessment for everyone"], 0,
     "Best: a charter explains the service and its limits. Why not the others: salary and patient details do not belong; an unkeepable guarantee becomes a broken promise."),
    ("A unit generates 15 hours of social work a week and 12 hours are assigned. What is the balance?",
     ["&minus;3 hours a week", "+3 hours a week", "0 hours", "&minus;27 hours"], 0,
     "Best: balance = available &minus; demand = 12 &minus; 15 = &minus;3. Why not the others: the sign or arithmetic is wrong."),
    ("You inherit 40 unseen referrals. How should you start?",
     ["Sort by risk: safety concerns today, threats to the next session this week, then the rest; report the backlog in writing", "See them in the order received", "See the families who complain loudest first", "Close the oldest ones as no longer relevant"], 0,
     "Best: triage by risk and make the shortfall visible. Why not the others: order of arrival or loudness ignores risk; closing without contact abandons people."),
    ("Which body issues the rules on who may practice social work in the Philippines?",
     ["Congress, through the social work law, with registration by the PRC", "The DOH", "PhilHealth", "The employer"], 0,
     "Best: Republic Act No. 4373 as amended, administered with the PRC. Why not the others: the DOH licenses facilities; PhilHealth sets benefits; employers set job descriptions."),
],
"04": [
    ("A patient stays silent while a relative answers every question. What does the silence prove?",
     ["Neither agreement nor incapacity; explore her communication and preferences privately", "That she cannot decide", "That she agrees with everything", "That she needs no services"], 0,
     "Best: silence has many meanings, including <em>hiya</em> or fear. Why not the others: each conclusion assumes what you have not assessed."),
    ("For a sensitive interview in the patient&rsquo;s regional language, whom should you prefer as interpreter?",
     ["A trained interpreter or uninvolved bilingual staff member with confidentiality arrangements", "Her grandchild", "Whoever is in the waiting area", "No one; use simple English"], 0,
     "Best: a neutral interpreter protects accuracy and privacy. Why not the others: children and interested relatives may filter answers; bystanders breach privacy; avoiding interpretation leaves her unheard."),
    ("Which is the safest opening question about family involvement?",
     ["&ldquo;Who would you like involved in this conversation?&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Your family must attend.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Who pays your bills?&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Why are you hiding things from your family?&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: it gives the choice to the patient. Why not the others: the others impose, presume payment equals authority, or accuse."),
    ("What should a culturally safe assessment do?",
     ["Ask what beliefs, practices, and supports matter to this person", "Infer beliefs from the person&rsquo;s religion", "Avoid discussing beliefs at all", "Assume all family decisions are consensual"], 0,
     "Best: culture is learned from the person, not assumed. Why not the others: inference stereotypes; avoidance misses strengths; consensus cannot be assumed."),
    ("Which question uses teach-back?",
     ["&ldquo;So I know I explained it well, can you tell me in your own words what happens next Monday?&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Do you understand everything?&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Why did you not listen?&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Will you sign now?&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: it checks understanding and puts the burden on the explanation. Why not the others: yes-no questions invite polite agreement; blame and pressure do not check understanding."),
    ("An older patient answers &ldquo;Opo&rdquo; to every explanation. What is the best interpretation?",
     ["It may mean respect or that she heard you, not that she understood; check with teach-back", "She clearly understood everything", "She is confused and cannot decide", "She does not want information"], 0,
     "Best: politeness and <em>hiya</em> can make it hard to admit confusion. Why not the others: each draws a conclusion you have not checked."),
    ("In the systematic review of health literacy in chronic kidney disease, about how common was limited health literacy among people on dialysis?",
     ["About 27%, mostly from United States studies of low to moderate quality", "Under 5%", "About 75%", "It was not measured"], 0,
     "Best: pooled prevalence was 27% on dialysis (25% overall), with caveats about study quality and setting. Why not the others: the figures are wrong or the claim is false."),
    ("How should you use the concept of <em>utang na loob</em> in an assessment?",
     ["As a prompt to ask whom the person feels able, or unable, to ask for help", "As proof that the family will always help", "As a reason the person cannot refuse donation", "Not at all; it is irrelevant"], 0,
     "Best: cultural concepts are questions to explore. Why not the others: they become stereotypes or excuses for pressure; ignoring them may miss real dynamics."),
    ("How do you see a patient alone without offending a relative who insists on staying?",
     ["Explain a rule that applies to everyone: you start every interview alone, then invite family", "Tell the relative you suspect abuse", "Interview them together and skip privacy", "Ask security to remove the relative"], 0,
     "Best: a universal rule protects privacy without accusing. Why not the others: accusation or force damages trust; skipping privacy loses safety and money questions."),
    ("What should you record after asking about family involvement?",
     ["Whom the patient wants involved, what may be shared, who speaks if they cannot, and the date", "Only the relative&rsquo;s phone number", "Nothing; it is informal", "The family&rsquo;s preferences only"], 0,
     "Best: a dated record lets the team follow the patient&rsquo;s choices. Why not the others: they miss the patient&rsquo;s choices or the record entirely."),
],
"05": [
    ("Which statement is a formulation?",
     ["&ldquo;Work and travel costs are blocking attendance, despite his stated wish to continue dialysis.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Patient is difficult.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Patient missed twice.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Patient needs a form.&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: a formulation explains why. Why not the others: a label, a fact without explanation, and a task are not formulations."),
    ("Family and patient accounts conflict. What should you do?",
     ["Record each with its source and explore the difference respectfully", "Hide the conflict", "Always favor whoever pays", "Use it to deny help"], 0,
     "Best: sourced accounts let the team weigh them. Why not the others: hiding, favoring payers, or penalizing the person are unfair and unsafe."),
    ("An assessment names a caregiver. What should you establish next?",
     ["Their actual availability, tasks, willingness, and limits", "Only their relationship to the patient", "Their social media accounts", "Whether they can replace the nurse"], 0,
     "Best: a named caregiver may have little capacity. Why not the others: relationship alone says nothing about capacity; social media is irrelevant; caregivers do not replace nurses."),
    ("What is a distress-screen result?",
     ["Information to combine with the interview and clinical referral", "A complete psychiatric diagnosis", "A substitute for consent", "A transplant eligibility verdict"], 0,
     "Best: a screen points where to look. Why not the others: it does not diagnose, replace consent, or decide transplant."),
    ("Which written note uses descriptive language?",
     ["&ldquo;Missed 4 of 13 sessions in October; reports work conflicts.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Noncompliant.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Difficult patient, poor attitude.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;In denial.&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: it states what happened and the stated reason. Why not the others: labels explain nothing and blame the person."),
    ("In the four Ps, which factor keeps a problem going?",
     ["Perpetuating", "Predisposing", "Precipitating", "Protective"], 0,
     "Best: perpetuating factors maintain the problem. Why not the others: predisposing makes vulnerable, precipitating triggers, protective helps."),
    ("Midway through an assessment the patient says, &ldquo;Sometimes I think it would be better if I were gone.&rdquo; What do you do?",
     ["Stop the assessment, ask directly about thoughts of ending his life, and follow the safety pathway", "Note it and finish the domains first", "Change the subject to avoid upsetting him", "Ask his wife later what he meant"], 0,
     "Best: a possible suicide signal comes before every domain. Why not the others: delay, avoidance, or asking someone else risks missing danger."),
    ("Why record a strength beside each barrier?",
     ["Because plans that use what a person already does well are more likely to be carried out", "Because it makes the report longer", "Because agencies require it", "Because barriers do not matter"], 0,
     "Best: strengths carry the plan. Why not the others: length and agency rules are not the reason; barriers still matter."),
    ("What does writing an &ldquo;Unknown&rdquo; line at the end of an assessment do?",
     ["Keeps the assessment honest and shows what to ask next", "Signals that the assessment failed", "Lets you fill gaps with assumptions later", "Is unnecessary if the form is complete"], 0,
     "Best: listing unknowns directs follow-up. Why not the others: unknowns are normal; assumptions must not fill them; a complete form can still hide unknowns."),
    ("Why should you check a formulation with the person?",
     ["It is a hypothesis; their correction is valuable information", "To get them to sign it", "Because the law requires it", "So they agree with the team&rsquo;s plan"], 0,
     "Best: the person knows their life best. Why not the others: signatures, legal claims, and persuasion are not the purpose."),
],
"06": [
    ("What does a sent referral mean?",
     ["Only that the request left; the outcome is not yet confirmed", "That the need is resolved", "That the agency granted funds", "That the patient attended"], 0,
     "Best: sent is the first of five stations. Why not the others: each assumes an outcome that has not been confirmed."),
    ("Which care plan action is strongest?",
     ["&ldquo;Social worker confirms an affordable ride before the next appointment, by Friday.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Improve motivation.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Find help someday.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Be more responsible.&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: it has an owner, a specific action, and a date. Why not the others: they are vague, untimed, or blaming."),
    ("An assistance agency declines a referral. What is the best next step?",
     ["Record the reason, tell the patient, and agree a feasible alternative", "Close it without telling the patient", "Mark it approved", "Send more private data to unrelated agencies"], 0,
     "Best: a decline with a reason closes the loop and guides the next step. Why not the others: silence, falsification, and oversharing harm the person."),
    ("Demand exceeds safe service capacity. What should you do?",
     ["Escalate the capacity and risk in writing with a prioritized coverage proposal", "Hide the backlog", "Stop seeing routine cases permanently", "Invent a legal staffing ratio"], 0,
     "Best: a visible, prioritized shortfall can be managed. Why not the others: hiding or abandoning work is unsafe; an invented ratio is false."),
    ("Which goal is written in the person&rsquo;s own terms?",
     ["&ldquo;Stop borrowing from the lender to pay for transport by January.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Address financial concerns.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Improve compliance.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Psychiatric evaluation.&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: it is specific, timed, and meaningful to the person. Why not the others: vague, blaming, or an action rather than a goal."),
    ("A referral was sent March 3, due March 13, and today is March 20. What does the aging tool show?",
     ["17 days open, 7 days overdue: overdue", "7 days open, on track", "17 days open, escalate", "10 days open, due soon"], 0,
     "Best: open = 20 &minus; 3 = 17 days; overdue = 20 &minus; 13 = 7 days, which is the overdue tier (escalate starts beyond 7). Why not the others: the arithmetic or tier is wrong."),
    ("At which station does a referral loop close?",
     ["When the person confirms the outcome reached them", "When it is sent", "When it is received", "When the agency decides"], 0,
     "Best: help is what reached the household. Why not the others: each earlier station is necessary but not sufficient."),
    ("What did the navigation trial in 23 hemodialysis units show?",
     ["Navigated patients completed more steps toward transplant (3.5 against 1.6), a process outcome", "Navigation doubled the number of transplants", "Navigation lowered mortality", "Navigation had no effect"], 0,
     "Best: the trial measured steps completed. Why not the others: it did not show more transplants or lower mortality, and it did show an effect on steps."),
    ("Which event should bring a care plan review forward?",
     ["A hospital admission or new missed sessions", "A routine monthly session", "A change of social worker&rsquo;s desk", "A holiday with no change in the patient&rsquo;s situation"], 0,
     "Best: admissions and missed sessions signal change. Why not the others: routine or unrelated events are not triggers."),
    ("When you step a case down, what must the person know?",
     ["How and why to come back, and whom to contact", "That the case is permanently closed", "Nothing; it is an internal decision", "That they no longer qualify for help"], 0,
     "Best: a clear way back is part of closure. Why not the others: closure is not abandonment and should be explained."),
],
"07": [
    ("What is a published annual hemodialysis benefit ceiling?",
     ["A policy-limited provider benefit, subject to its conditions", "Cash paid directly to the patient", "A personal savings account", "Proof that every expense is covered"], 0,
     "Best: benefits are paid under conditions, usually to providers. Why not the others: they misdescribe what a benefit is."),
    ("Who determines the final payer outcome of a claim?",
     ["The payer, through its process", "The social worker", "The course website", "The patient&rsquo;s employer"], 0,
     "Best: PhilHealth decides claims. Why not the others: none of these has that authority."),
    ("A new transplant circular has just been published. For an existing case, what should you do first?",
     ["Check its effectivity, the case dates, and the transition provisions with the contracted provider", "Apply all the new rules at once", "Cancel the prior authorization", "Promise the highest rate"], 0,
     "Best: publication is not effectivity, and older cases may follow older rules. Why not the others: each skips the checks or makes a promise."),
    ("A family disputes a dialysis charge. What should it trigger?",
     ["An itemized review against the relevant benefit rule, through the authorized route", "Altering the attendance record", "Advice to stop dialysis", "A promise of reimbursement"], 0,
     "Best: check the charge against the rule with the claims officer. Why not the others: falsification, unsafe advice, and promises are wrong."),
    ("Under PC 2024-0023 as officially explained, how many hemodialysis sessions are covered a year?",
     ["Up to 156", "Unlimited", "90", "52"], 0,
     "Best: up to 156 sessions a year, per the circular and Tamang Sagot. Why not the others: the numbers are wrong; the benefit is not unlimited."),
    ("A social media post says dialysis is &ldquo;100% free, all year, no limits.&rdquo; What is accurate?",
     ["There is no copayment for package-covered services, but the benefit is limited to 156 sessions a year and some services may cost money", "The post is completely accurate", "PhilHealth covers nothing", "Only public hospitals have no copayment"], 0,
     "Best: the official explanation covers no copayment for package services in public and private facilities, within a session limit. Why not the others: they overstate, understate, or misstate the rule."),
    ("A patient has used 120 sessions by September 30 on three sessions a week. About when will the 156 run out?",
     ["In about 12 weeks, around December 23", "In 36 days", "In about 3 weeks", "They will not run out this year"], 0,
     "Best: 156 &minus; 120 = 36 sessions; 36 &divide; 3 = 12 weeks. Why not the others: the arithmetic is wrong."),
    ("The peritoneal dialysis circular could not be fully retrieved. How should you discuss package amounts?",
     ["Do not quote amounts; check the current circular and the contracted provider", "Estimate from the hemodialysis rate", "Use an amount from a social media post", "Say it is free forever"], 0,
     "Best: unverified amounts must not be quoted. Why not the others: estimates and rumors become false promises."),
    ("Which line best documents a benefit explanation?",
     ["&ldquo;Explained per PC 2024-0023 and its Tamang Sagot, checked on [date].&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Explained PhilHealth.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Told patient it is covered.&rdquo;", "&ldquo;Benefit OK.&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: naming the version and date makes the advice checkable and correctable. Why not the others: they do not say which rule was used."),
    ("A daughter asks how much PhilHealth will pay for her father&rsquo;s transplant. What is the best answer?",
     ["Show the circular and the facility&rsquo;s written estimate; do not promise an amount", "Give the highest amount you have heard", "Say PhilHealth pays everything", "Say you cannot help with money questions"], 0,
     "Best: point to the official source and the provider, without promising. Why not the others: promises and refusals both fail her."),
],
"08": [
    ("How should a guarantee letter be treated?",
     ["As a commitment whose scope and the provider&rsquo;s acceptance need confirming", "As proof that all expenses are settled", "As cash already received", "As permission to share the whole chart"], 0,
     "Best: a guarantee letter covers a stated amount for a stated provider. Why not the others: it is not total coverage, cash, or a privacy waiver."),
    ("What should an assistance directory contain?",
     ["Eligibility, access route, requirements, what it pays for, and the date it was verified", "Agency names only", "A promised amount for every patient", "Patients&rsquo; passwords"], 0,
     "Best: dated, specific information sends families to the right place. Why not the others: names alone, promises, or credentials are useless or harmful."),
    ("Which assistance summary is strongest?",
     ["A factual account of assessed need, resources, verified expenses, and the remaining gap", "A dramatic account with invented debts", "A case copied from another patient", "A request to ignore eligibility rules"], 0,
     "Best: honest, specific reports are credible. Why not the others: invention and copying are false documentation."),
    ("A request is denied. What should you do?",
     ["Explore alternatives and immediate access risks, and explain what remains unresolved", "Guarantee that the next agency will pay", "Mark the request as paid", "Recommend any lender without assessment"], 0,
     "Best: keep treatment safe and look for another route. Why not the others: promises, falsification, and unassessed loans harm the person."),
    ("An office asks for a letter saying the patient &ldquo;is dying&rdquo; when that is not true. What do you do?",
     ["Decline to write anything untrue and offer a factual social case study", "Write it, since it helps the patient", "Ask the nephrologist to write it", "Tell the family to find another social worker"], 0,
     "Best: false documentation harms the person and every future applicant. Why not the others: it remains false whoever writes it; sending them away abandons them."),
    ("Total cost this month is PHP 12,000, confirmed support PHP 6,000, and a local request is pending. What is the gap?",
     ["PHP 6,000 until the local decision arrives", "PHP 0, assuming the local request is approved", "PHP 12,000", "PHP 18,000"], 0,
     "Best: subtract only confirmed support. Why not the others: they count pending support, ignore confirmed support, or add instead of subtract."),
    ("Which source is usually not available in a private freestanding dialysis unit?",
     ["A Malasakit Center desk", "DSWD crisis assistance through a field office", "PCSO medical assistance by application", "Local government assistance"], 0,
     "Best: Malasakit Centers are in DOH hospitals and designated hospitals. Why not the others: those programs are reached through their own offices."),
    ("A politician&rsquo;s staff offer to pay a balance if the family appears in a photo. What do you do?",
     ["Ask the family privately, make refusal safe, and help them set limits if they agree", "Accept on their behalf", "Refuse on their behalf", "Post the photo yourself"], 0,
     "Best: the choice is theirs, made freely. Why not the others: deciding for them removes their choice."),
    ("Why track applications to their decisions instead of counting submissions?",
     ["Because only decided and received support helps the household", "Because agencies require it", "Because submissions are always approved", "Because it makes reports longer"], 0,
     "Best: help is what arrives. Why not the others: they misstate the purpose or the facts."),
    ("What should you list before choosing assistance sources?",
     ["The household&rsquo;s costs for the next three months", "Every agency in the region", "The patient&rsquo;s full medical history", "Other families&rsquo; amounts received"], 0,
     "Best: start from the costs, then match sources. Why not the others: they are unfocused, excessive, or private."),
],
"09": [
    ("When is a transport plan incomplete?",
     ["When it covers only the outward trip although the return is the problem", "When it names a backup", "When it includes mobility needs", "When it records a contact"], 0,
     "Best: the return after dialysis, when people are weak, is often the hardest part. Why not the others: those are parts of a good plan."),
    ("How should an employer letter be handled?",
     ["With the patient&rsquo;s consent, disclosing only what the employer needs", "Send the full diagnosis automatically", "Promise the patient will keep the job", "Replace the clinician&rsquo;s medical certificate"], 0,
     "Best: consent and minimum disclosure. Why not the others: they overshare, overpromise, or exceed your role."),
    ("A patient shortens sessions to catch a cheap ride. What is the best response?",
     ["Tell the clinical team and work on the travel barrier together", "Praise the efficient schedule", "Recommend fewer sessions", "Record only &ldquo;noncompliant&rdquo;"], 0,
     "Best: shortened sessions are clinical; the ride is your problem to solve. Why not the others: praise or advice is unsafe; a label solves nothing."),
    ("What does an access cost worksheet establish?",
     ["A transparent estimate with stated assumptions", "An official bill", "A payer entitlement", "Automatic grant eligibility"], 0,
     "Best: it is planning arithmetic. Why not the others: it creates no bill, entitlement, or eligibility."),
    ("Six visits at PHP 120 out, 120 back, 0 escort, and 30 other, plus PHP 300 one-time, with PHP 1,000 confirmed support. What is the gap?",
     ["PHP 920", "PHP 1,920", "PHP 0", "PHP 620"], 0,
     "Best: 6 &times; 270 + 300 = 1,920; 1,920 &minus; 1,000 = 920. Why not the others: they ignore support or miscalculate."),
    ("Household health spending is PHP 4,000 of PHP 25,000 total spending. What is the share, and what does it mean?",
     ["16%, above the 10% SDG 3.8.2 mark, a population hardship indicator, not an eligibility rule", "16%, which qualifies the family for assistance", "6.25%, below concern", "25%, the catastrophic mark"], 0,
     "Best: 4,000 &divide; 25,000 = 16%; the marks describe hardship, not program eligibility. Why not the others: wrong arithmetic or wrong meaning."),
    ("What did the DOPPS travel time study find?",
     ["Longer travel time was associated with higher mortality and lower quality of life, in an observational analysis", "Travel time had no relationship with outcomes", "Shorter travel caused worse outcomes", "Travel time was proven to cause deaths"], 0,
     "Best: an association from observational data with a single unvalidated question. Why not the others: they contradict or overstate the finding."),
    ("A patient says the family eats rice and instant noodles &ldquo;to save for the fare.&rdquo; What should you do?",
     ["Tell the dietitian and nephrologist this week and work on the access cost", "Advise more noodles for energy", "Note it as a personal choice", "Wait until weight loss appears"], 0,
     "Best: food insecurity is a clinical risk (salt, weight loss) with a social cause. Why not the others: advice is unsafe; waiting lets harm happen."),
    ("What should you ask every patient on dialysis about the PWD ID?",
     ["Whether they have one, and help them apply if they may qualify", "Nothing; it is not related to dialysis", "Whether they want to pay extra for it", "Whether their family has one instead"], 0,
     "Best: people on dialysis may qualify as persons with disability (PWD), and the ID carries a legal discount. Why not the others: they miss or misdescribe the benefit."),
    ("A patient lost his driving job after starting dialysis. Which step could recover income?",
     ["Check his SSS contributions for sickness or disability benefits", "Tell him to stop dialysis to work", "Assume he cannot work again", "Ask the unit to pay him"], 0,
     "Best: benefits from past contributions may apply. Why not the others: unsafe, defeatist, or impossible."),
],
"10": [
    ("What does a screening score establish by itself?",
     ["Neither a diagnosis nor all of the urgency; it points where to look", "A psychiatric diagnosis", "That an interview is unnecessary", "That any staff member may treat"], 0,
     "Best: a screen such as the Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9) is the start of assessment. Why not the others: they overstate what a score can do."),
    ("Sudden confusion appears during a session. What should happen?",
     ["Clinical assessment for an acute cause, now", "Reassurance only", "A routine form next month", "An assumption of depression"], 0,
     "Best: acute confusion can signal a medical emergency. Why not the others: they delay or misattribute it."),
    ("You lack training in a specialized psychotherapy. What should you do?",
     ["Work within your competence and refer, or seek qualified supervision", "Provide it because the course mentions counseling", "Invent a local certificate", "Avoid all supportive contact"], 0,
     "Best: supportive work is yours; specialized therapy needs training. Why not the others: overreach, falsification, or withdrawal."),
    ("The PHQ-9 total is low, but the patient discloses immediate intent to harm himself. What is the priority?",
     ["Immediate safety response and clinical escalation", "The low total", "Rescreening next year", "Finishing the questionnaire"], 0,
     "Best: a disclosure overrides any total. Why not the others: they let the number hide the danger."),
    ("Items scored: interest 2, mood 2, sleep 3, energy 3, appetite 2, failure 2, concentration 1, slowness 1, item 9 = 0. What is the total and its meaning?",
     ["16; depression likely enough to report and refer; not a diagnosis", "16; major depression is confirmed", "14; minimal symptoms", "16; the safety pathway is required"], 0,
     "Best: the sum is 16, above the dialysis cutoff of 10; item 9 is 0. Why not the others: a score is not a diagnosis; 14 is wrong; the safety pathway follows item 9, not the total."),
    ("By clinical interview, about how common was depression among people on dialysis in the Palmer meta-analysis?",
     ["About 23%; rating scales gave higher figures (about 39%)", "About 5%", "About 75%", "It was the same by every method"], 0,
     "Best: 22.8% by interview, 39.3% by scales, with scales likely overestimating. Why not the others: wrong figures or method."),
    ("Which PHQ-9 items overlap most with symptoms of kidney failure?",
     ["Sleep, energy, and appetite", "Interest and low mood", "Feeling a failure", "Item 9"], 0,
     "Best: uremia, anemia, and itch affect sleep, energy, and appetite. Why not the others: those are core mood items the kidneys do not explain."),
    ("What did the Cochrane review find about cognitive behavioral therapy for depression in dialysis?",
     ["It probably improves depressive symptoms, with moderate-certainty evidence", "It is proven to lower mortality", "It has no effect", "It is harmful"], 0,
     "Best: moderate-certainty improvement in symptom scores. Why not the others: survival was not shown; benefit was found; no harm was reported as the finding."),
    ("What did ASCEND compare and find?",
     ["Sertraline versus chairside CBT; clinician-rated scores were modestly lower with sertraline, with more side effects, and no placebo group", "Sertraline versus placebo; sertraline cured depression", "CBT versus no treatment; CBT lowered mortality", "Exercise versus medicine; exercise was better"], 0,
     "Best: an open-label comparison of two active treatments. Why not the others: they misdescribe the trial."),
    ("A patient declines a mental health referral. What should you do?",
     ["Respect the decision, record it, keep the door open, and rescreen later", "Refer anyway without telling him", "Tell the family to insist", "Close the case"], 0,
     "Best: patients decide for their own reasons; your role is to offer and stay available. Why not the others: they override or abandon him."),
],
}
